course: dictionary
lesson: 2732
title: Dire: serious, urgent or just awful
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is right. Dire is one of these words. «The village faced a dire warning about the flood.» A warning about a flood is serious and dangerous, so dire means very serious or terrible here.
explain: «The camp was in dire need of food and water.» The words need, food and water tell you that help is urgent. Dire can mean extremely urgent. You will also meet set phrases such as «dire straits», which means a very difficult situation, so it is worth learning them as a group.
explain: In everyday UK talk, dire is informal for very bad: «The film was absolutely dire.» Nobody is in danger there; the film was just awful. Ask yourself: does the sentence mention danger or a serious problem? If yes, dire means serious. If not, it may mean bad.
fact: Dire means very serious or terrible, often dangerous.
fact: Dire needs and situations are extremely urgent, as in «in dire need of help».
fact: In UK informal speech, dire means very bad or low quality, as in «The meal was dire».
fact: The comparative forms are direr and direst.
fact: In 2025 a company was reported to have made wolf pups to look like the extinct dire wolf, but they are grey wolves with white fur and a few changed genes, and scientists disagree about calling it de-extinction.
exercise: «The town was in dire need of clean water after the flood.» What does dire mean here? | Slightly wet | *Extremely urgent | Dark and quiet | Very cheap
exercise: Ava says, «That cartoon was absolutely dire.» What does dire mean here? | Brand new | *Very bad or low quality | Very quiet | Very serious and dangerous, like a storm
exercise: «The warning was dire: stay away from the cliff edge.» What does dire mean here? | Very funny | Rude and short | *Very serious and dangerous | Long and boring
test: In «The family were in dire straits after losing their home», what does dire straits mean? | *A very difficult situation | A long wait | A narrow street | A kind of boat
test: Which sentence uses dire to mean awful, not dangerous? | «The dire warning told everyone to leave.» | «The storm brought dire consequences.» | *«The pizza was dire, cold and soggy.» | «They were in dire need of rescue.»
exam: Why does dire mean something different in «The dire forecast warns of floods» and «The dinner was dire»? | Forecast and dinner are both verbs, so dire changes | *Context shows danger in the first and that the dinner was awful in the second | Dire is a place name in the second sentence | It means exactly the same in both, so the context does not matter
